WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:05.370
A significant hurdle when authoring UI with a code backend is that I'm grabbing parts

2
00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:09.660
of the visuals that don't yet exist. For instance, how can we update a text field

3
00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.640
in code when the field itself was added later in Blueprint, because we won't have

4
00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:17.620
a reference to it? Luckily, Unreal presents two exceptionally

5
00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:22.100
important new property specifiers to empower the UI programmer, that being the bind widget

6
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:26.339
and the bind widget anim. Let's take a look at how we'd go about

7
00:00:26.339 --> 00:00:30.339
using them, and what functions we'd bring in around them to develop more smarter pieces

8
00:00:30.339 --> 00:00:34.649
of UI. Bind widget is a meta specifier intended for

9
00:00:34.649 --> 00:00:39.549
use with widget pointers in our user widget classes, and allows a designer to populate

10
00:00:39.549 --> 00:00:45.090
native C++ level members with a designer created widget. This does kind of fly in the

11
00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:49.409
face of the general Unreal development pipeline that have a blueprint layer sitting on top

12
00:00:49.409 --> 00:00:54.929
of a C++ one. In this case, we're essentially leaving a gap in the C++ layer for a blueprint

13
00:00:54.929 --> 00:00:59.899
layer to fill it in for us. Let's talk through a practical example.

14
00:00:59.899 --> 00:01:05.260
Let's say we've got a C++ class based off of user widget with a uTextBlock member.

15
00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.959
The uTextBlock container is exactly what you'd think it is, it's a widget capable

16
00:01:08.959 --> 00:01:14.150
of rendering text in UMG. If we flag this up with the bind widget

17
00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:17.590
specifier, we're saying to the compiler that this particular widget is populated

18
00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:21.430
from the designer layer. We don't need to initialise it in the native code like

19
00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:26.379
we would with an actor component. This frees up any UI designers to implement the text

20
00:01:26.379 --> 00:01:30.459
block for us and change its visuals, its positions, its parenting, and any other parameters

21
00:01:30.459 --> 00:01:35.450
that they may need to adjust as per the design. As engineers, we're now free to assume

22
00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:39.969
that this field is now valid and can carry on developing as we would otherwise.

23
00:01:39.969 --> 00:01:43.609
But of course, it is a pointer, so validity checks are still always a pretty smart idea.

24
00:01:43.609 --> 00:01:48.290
But you may be asking yourself, why can we assume it's valid? Well, the blueprint

25
00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:53.290
will fail to compile if it's not compliant with the underlying native code.

26
00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:57.549
Bind widget is signaling to the blueprint layer, hey, I need a widget of this type

27
00:01:57.549 --> 00:02:02.189
with this specific name to properly work, so you need to add one.

28
00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:06.349
Failure to comply will manifest much like any other blueprint compilation failure.

29
00:02:06.349 --> 00:02:10.289
A warning error will be thrown up as soon as we try and play the game in the editor,

30
00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:14.610
and a whole host of errors will be thrown into the log, so it's not a good time.

31
00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:18.009
It's worth noting that the bind widget will happily bind to an existing widget as

32
00:02:18.009 --> 00:02:22.009
long as it matches in name and in type. The compiler isn't looking for the creation

33
00:02:22.009 --> 00:02:27.020
of such a widget, it's merely looking for the existence of one. Once we compile with

34
00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:31.419
a compliant widget in the designer, the blueprint compiler is going to populate the underlying

35
00:02:31.419 --> 00:02:37.379
C++ with the designer version of the widget, filling any gaps we may have deliberately left.

36
00:02:37.379 --> 00:02:40.840
At this point, we've not actually got any widgets in the viewport, we're still

37
00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:44.639
just looking at the designer. Why would we need any widgets to be passed

38
00:02:44.639 --> 00:02:48.250
down into the native code when we're not actually running the game?

39
00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:52.810
This is where our smart UI elements come into play.

40
00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:57.129
SynchronizeProperties is a virtual function that we can override to develop smart, responsive

41
00:02:57.129 --> 00:03:03.379
UI tools. In many ways, it mirrors the on-construction or post-edit property virtuals we typically

42
00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:07.659
use when authoring smart actor classes. It will be called every time a property changes

43
00:03:07.659 --> 00:03:12.500
within a specified widget in both the designer and at runtime.

44
00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:16.219
Because it's called both at runtime and in the designer, we more often than not want

45
00:03:16.219 --> 00:03:20.060
to branch the logic inside depending on the current environment.

46
00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:25.539
Wrapping logic in a isDesignTime check means we can quite easily run design-only

47
00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:30.860
So let's take a look at a practical example of how we'd use BindWidget in combination

48
00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:35.319
with SynchronizeProperties to empower the UI designers as well as ourselves to build

49
00:03:35.319 --> 00:03:40.180
more complex pieces of UI. In this example, we're implementing the

50
00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:45.169
SynchronizeProperties function and supering it back up the chain so there's nothing special there.

51
00:03:45.169 --> 00:03:48.889
We're then checking to see if we're in the designer, and if so, we check to see

52
00:03:48.889 --> 00:03:54.900
if the container widget is valid. containerWidget in this instance is a BindWidget property.

53
00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.819
If we've yet to create a valid widget in the designer for this property to bind to,

54
00:03:58.819 --> 00:04:02.000
when we compile we're going to hit a null pointer.

55
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.599
If it does exist, however, we're going to remove all the children that are inside so

56
00:04:05.599 --> 00:04:09.199
we've got a fresh container to work with before proceeding through our dynamic spawn

57
00:04:09.199 --> 00:04:14.030
of as many children as the child number is set to.

58
00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:17.709
Of course this is a very simplistic example but even here we can see the implications

59
00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:21.139
of real authoring. The alternative would be a designer manually

60
00:04:21.139 --> 00:04:24.980
creating a vertical box and dragging in as many images as they want directly.

61
00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:28.949
Why have the specifications changed and we need to increase the number of images?

62
00:04:28.949 --> 00:04:31.269
Suddenly we're dealing with a less than trivial UI rework.

63
00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:36.379
With smart and sensible use of SynchronizeProperties, we can author responsive pieces

64
00:04:36.379 --> 00:04:39.899
of UI that empower the team as opposed to laboring them with more work than is

65
00:04:39.899 --> 00:04:45.019
strictly necessary. bindWidgetAnim allows us to grab named

66
00:04:45.019 --> 00:04:49.259
animations from the designer much in the same way that we grab widgets when using the bind widget.

67
00:04:50.139 --> 00:04:55.660
When defining animations in native C++, we simply create a member of type UWidgetAnimation

68
00:04:55.699 --> 00:04:58.819
and use the name of the animation to create the underlying binding.

69
00:05:00.379 --> 00:05:04.379
When we want the animation to play, it's as simple as calling the playAnimation from within

70
00:05:04.379 --> 00:05:10.290
our user widget, passing along the bound animation. Much like bindWidget, a failure to

71
00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:14.129
bind to an animation of a particular name will also cause a compilation failure.

72
00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:18.560
We'll also fail to compile if the widget animation isn't marked with the transient

73
00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:23.949
specifier along with the bindWidgetAnim meta. This is primarily due to how widgets

74
00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:28.269
themselves play animations under the hood. During the compilation, the animations themselves

75
00:05:28.269 --> 00:05:32.269
get copied out of the user widget into a holding container as part of the transient package.

76
00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:37.069
Marking the animations as transient prevents these copied versions from being serialized.
